  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 327 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nSeasonably cool temperatures are expected for the rest of the \nweek. Tuesday night lows across the region are expected to be in\nthe low to mid 50s. However, the NBM indicates there is a \n30-50% chance that our northern area could drop in the upper \n40s. This is possible by having an anomalously high surface \nridge of 1036mb (>99% climatological percentile) over the \neastern Great Lakes and southeastern Canada. Models are \nconfident that this air mass will continue to dominate for the \nrest of the week with a gradual warming through the 70s, \nreaching the low 80s by next weekend. NBM and LREF guidance has \ninterquartile ranges of around 5 degrees for each day this week \nand into the weekend.\n\nThe GFS and EPS ensembles both show an upper and mid-level ridge\nbuilding over the southeastern Canada region with a center of\nlow pressure over the Ohio Valley on Wednesday. The ridge should\nremain fairly stationary over southeastern Canada, while the low\npressure center will slowly move to the east coast of the CONUS.\nThis pattern will allow for continued westerly and northwesterly\nflow aloft and northerly winds at the surface to help moderate\nour temperatures into next weekend and limit the moisture\ntransported into the region. This will prevent another return of\nthe heat and humidity in the foreseeable future; also limiting\nthe chances for further rainfall during the coming week.",
